#By default, when you add a new linux user to the system, i.e. /etc/passwd and /etc/shadow files. 
#The system grants shell access to the user. 
#If using POP3 or SMTP or FTP/SFTP, the new account doesnt need shell access.
#Best way to restrict shell access is to use a special shell called nologin. this shell politely refuses logins.
#it is intended as a replacement shell field for system accounts that have been disabled or have other user-level
#access such as ftp, pop3,smtp etc.
#This is a common practise followed by isp or web hosting service provider's web, mail and FTP server(s).


#full path using type or command commands
type nologin
command -V nologin

#ensure that /etc/shells file contains nologin, else services such as ftp may not allow login.
#if not append nologin to it with
